seg-10c7eaab58c5 = Hardware safety: what Kshana does with hardware, and what it never does

# text
seg-fe4873962140 = <b>Status: owner legal review pending.</b> The legal notes below are the maintainers' reading of public sources, written so a user knows where the limits are. They are not legal advice, they have not been reviewed by counsel, and the law differs by country. Read them as a reason to ask a lawyer, not as a permission.

# text
seg-bd2fbb355834 = Kshana is advisory software for research, simulation and test. It is not type-approved equipment and does not rate or approve a receiver, a clock or a laboratory. See the advisory statement in the README.

# text
seg-f27c286787b1 = The rule

# text
seg-88638e41faab = Receive is the product. Transmitting is never in the open engine.

# text
seg-614d381dd0d0 = Kshana contains no code that drives a transmitter. There is no SDR transmit path (no UHD, HackRF, bladeRF or Soapy transmit call), and no jamming or spoofing waveform is written to a file meant for playback.

# text
# c1: .sdrx
# c2: kshana iq scene
# c3: .sdrx
seg-2e370a1c6c4c = Files Kshana writes (IQ scenes, SigMF and ION <c1/> recordings, NMEA and bench exports) describe <b>simulated, legitimate</b> signals or motion, and the metadata Kshana writes for a scene says it was written by <c2/> (the ION <c3/> comment says it is a simulated scene, not a recording).

# text
seg-8ce652bc1af6 = Where an experiment needs RF on a bench, the <b>operator's own equipment</b> produces it, in the operator's own enclosure, under the operator's own authorisation. Kshana records what the operator says was done, and never infers it.

